    31	---
    32	
    33	## Statement
    34	
    35	Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a, b > 0$ and let $r, s \in \mathbb{Q}$, with
    36	rational powers as in [[def-rational-power]]. Then:
    37	
    38	1. $a^{r} > 0$.
    39	2. $a^{r+s} = a^{r} a^{s}$.
    40	3. $(ab)^{r} = a^{r} b^{r}$; in particular $\big(ab\big)^{1/N} = a^{1/N} b^{1/N}$ for every natural $N \ge 1$.
    41	4. $a^{-r} = \big(a^{r}\big)^{-1} = 1/a^{r}$.
    42	5. $\big(a^{r}\big)^{s} = a^{rs}$.
    43	
    44	Claims 2 and 3 persist in the supplementary case of [[def-rational-power]]: for
    45	$a, b \ge 0$ and rationals $r, s > 0$ ([[def-rat-order]]) one still has
    46	$(ab)^{r} = a^{r} b^{r}$ and $a^{r+s} = a^{r} a^{s}$. The two identities degenerate
    47	differently, and it is worth saying how. In the product identity, a zero base on
    48	either side makes both sides $0$. In the addition identity only the base $a$
    49	occurs, so it degenerates only when $a = 0$, and then both sides are $0$; when
    50	$a > 0$ that identity holds with no hypothesis on $b$ at all.
    51	
    52	## Facts & Assumptions
    53	
    54	**Given:** Reals $a, b > 0$ and rationals $r, s$.
    55	
    56	[L1] Definition and well-definedness ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-well-defined]]): for ANY representative $r = m/N$ with $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $N \ge 1$ natural, $a^{r} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}$; and $a^{1/N}$ is the unique $x \ge 0$ with $x^{N} = a$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]), which is $> 0$ when $a > 0$.
    57	
    58	[L2] Laws of integer exponents ([[lem-power-laws]], [[def-integer-power]]): for $x, y \ne 0$ and integers $j, k$, $x^{j+k} = x^{j}x^{k}$, $(x^{j})^{k} = x^{jk}$, $(xy)^{j} = x^{j}y^{j}$ and $x^{-j} = (x^{j})^{-1}$.
    59	
    60	[L3] Positivity and injectivity: $x > 0$ implies $x^{j} > 0$ for every NATURAL $j$ ([[lem-power-monotone]], claim 1), and hence for every integer $j$, since $x^{-j} = \big(x^{j}\big)^{-1}$ ([[lem-power-laws]], claim 2) and the inverse of a positive element is positive ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]); and $x \mapsto x^{N}$ is injective on $\{x \ge 0\}$ for $N \ge 1$ ([[lem-power-monotone]], claim 2).
    61	
    62	[L4] Rational arithmetic ([[def-rat-operations]], [[def-rationals]]): any two rationals can be written with a common positive denominator, $m/N + k/N = (m+k)/N$, $-(m/N) = (-m)/N$, and $(m/n)(p/q) = (mp)/(nq)$.
    63	
    64	[L5] The order on $\mathbb{Q}$ ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]], [[def-rat-order]]) is compatible with addition, so $r > 0$ and $s > 0$ imply $r + s > 0$.
    65	
    66	[L6] The supplementary clause of [[def-rational-power]]: $0^{t} = 0$ for every rational $t > 0$, while $0^{t}$ is left undefined for rational $t < 0$ and the convention $0^{0} = 1$ of [[def-integer-power]] is untouched. In a field, a product with a factor $0$ is $0$ ([[lem-of-zero-mult]]).
    67	
    68	## Proof
    69	
    70	**Proof technique:** direct.
    71	
    72	1.1 Choose a common denominator: there are a natural $N \ge 1$ and integers $m, k$ with $r = m/N$ and $s = k/N$; then $r + s = (m+k)/N$ and $-r = (-m)/N$. [L4]
    73	
    74	1.2 Roots of a product: for $a, b > 0$ and $N \ge 1$ the element $a^{1/N} b^{1/N}$ is positive and satisfies $\big(a^{1/N} b^{1/N}\big)^{N} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{N}\big(b^{1/N}\big)^{N} = ab$, so by uniqueness of the nonnegative $N$-th root $\big(ab\big)^{1/N} = a^{1/N} b^{1/N}$. [L1, L2, L3]
    75	
    76	2.1 Claim 1: $a^{r} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}$ with $a^{1/N} > 0$, and a positive element has positive integer powers, so $a^{r} > 0$. [step 1.1, L1, L3]
    77	
    78	2.2 Claim 3: $(ab)^{r} = \big((ab)^{1/N}\big)^{m} = \big(a^{1/N} b^{1/N}\big)^{m} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}\big(b^{1/N}\big)^{m} = a^{r} b^{r}$, using the root-of-a-product identity and then the integer product law. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L1, L2]
    79	
    80	3.1 Claim 2: $a^{r+s} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m+k} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}\big(a^{1/N}\big)^{k} = a^{r} a^{s}$, the middle equality being the integer addition law applied to the nonzero base $a^{1/N}$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2]
    81	
    82	3.2 Claim 4: $a^{-r} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{-m} = \Big(\big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}\Big)^{-1} = \big(a^{r}\big)^{-1}$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2]
    83	
    84	3.3 Claim 5: write $r = m/n$ and $s = p/q$ with $n, q \ge 1$, and put $x := a^{1/(nq)}$, so $x > 0$ and $x^{nq} = a$; then $\big(x^{q}\big)^{n} = x^{qn} = a$ with $x^{q} > 0$, so $x^{q} = a^{1/n}$ by uniqueness of the nonnegative $n$-th root; putting $z := x^{m}$ we get $z > 0$ and $z^{q} = \big(x^{m}\big)^{q} = \big(x^{q}\big)^{m} = \big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m} = a^{r}$, so $z$ is the nonnegative $q$-th root of $a^{r}$, that is $z = \big(a^{r}\big)^{1/q}$; therefore $\big(a^{r}\big)^{s} = \Big(\big(a^{r}\big)^{1/q}\Big)^{p} = z^{p} = \big(x^{m}\big)^{p} = x^{mp} = \big(a^{1/(nq)}\big)^{mp} = a^{(mp)/(nq)} = a^{rs}$. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L3, L4]
    85	
    86	3.4 The supplementary nonnegative case, product identity: let $a, b \ge 0$ and let $r > 0$ be rational; if $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ this is step 2.2, and otherwise $a = 0$ or $b = 0$, so $ab = 0$ and the left side is $0^{r} = 0$, while the right side $a^{r}b^{r}$ has a factor $0^{r} = 0$ and is therefore $0$ as well. [step 2.2, L6]
    87	
    88	4.1 The supplementary nonnegative case, addition identity: the identity $a^{r+s} = a^{r}a^{s}$ involves the base $a$ only, so nothing need be assumed about $b$; for $a > 0$ it is step 3.1 verbatim, which uses only $a > 0$, and both sides are then positive rather than $0$; for $a = 0$ the exponents satisfy $r + s > 0$, so the left side is $0^{r+s} = 0$ and the right side is $0 \cdot 0 = 0$. [step 3.1, L5, L6]
    89	
    90	5.1 All five claims hold for positive bases and arbitrary rational exponents, together with the two supplementary identities for nonnegative bases and positive rational exponents. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, step 2.2, step 3.3, step 3.4, step 4.1] ∎
