    31	---
    32	
    33	## Definition
    34	
    35	Let $a \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a > 0$ and let $r \in \mathbb{Q}$ ([[def-rationals]]).
    36	
    37	Every rational has a representative with positive denominator
    38	([[lem-rat-positive-denominator]]), so write $r = m/n$ with $m \in \mathbb{Z}$
    39	and $n$ a positive integer; a positive integer is the image of a unique natural
    40	$\ge 1$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]), and we write $n$ for that natural too. Define
    41	
    42	$$a^{r} := \big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m},$$
    43	
    44	where $a^{1/n}$ is the unique nonnegative $n$-th root of $a$
    45	([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]) and the outer exponent is an integer power
    46	([[def-integer-power]]). The outer power is legitimate because $a^{1/n} > 0$ when
    47	$a > 0$, so it has an inverse and negative integer exponents are allowed.
    48	
    49	**Well-definedness.** The right-hand side must not depend on which representative
    50	$m/n$ of $r$ was chosen. It does not: that is [[lem-rational-power-well-defined]],
    51	which is recorded in this item's `justified_by` rather than in its `deps`, since
    52	it is a statement *about* the operation defined here and therefore depends on
    53	this definition.
    54	
    55	**The base must be positive.** For $a < 0$ the same formula is not a definition
    56	at all, because different representatives of the same rational give different
    57	answers, or no answer: see [[fs-rational-power-of-negative-base]], which is
    58	exactly the item that justifies the restriction.
    59	
    60	**Supplementary clause for the base $0$.** For $a = 0$ and rational $r > 0$
    61	([[def-rat-order]]) the displayed formula still makes sense and still does not
    62	depend on the representative: $r > 0$ forces $m \ge 1$, and
    63	$\big(0^{1/n}\big)^{m} = 0^{m} = 0$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]] applies to every
    64	$a \ge 0$). So we set $0^{r} = 0$ for rational $r > 0$. For $r < 0$ the
    65	expression $0^{r}$ is left undefined, since $0$ has no inverse. This clause is
    66	what lets the inequalities later on this page be stated for nonnegative entries
    67	rather than for positive ones only.
    68	
    69	## Remarks
    70	
    71	- **Consistency with integer powers.** If $r \in \mathbb{Z}$ then $r = r/1$,
    72	  $a^{1/1} = a$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]), and the definition returns
    73	  $(a^{1})^{r} = a^{r}$ as given by [[def-integer-power]]. So the notation $a^{r}$
    74	  is unambiguous, and rational powers extend integer powers on positive bases.
    75	  At $r = 1/n$ it returns $\big(a^{1/n}\big)^{1} = a^{1/n}$, so the root notation
    76	  of [[thm-nth-roots-exist]] is the special case $r = 1/n$, as intended.
    77	- $a^{r} > 0$ for every $a > 0$ and **every** $r \in \mathbb{Q}$, the exponent $0$
    78	  included. Writing $r = m/n$ with $n \ge 1$: the root $a^{1/n}$ is positive
    79	  ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]); for $m \ge 0$ the value $\big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m}$ is a
    80	  natural power of a positive element, hence positive, which is claim 1 of
    81	  [[lem-power-monotone]] and covers $m = 0$ as well, since $a^{0} = 1 > 0$; and for
    82	  $m < 0$ the value is $\big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m} = \Big(\big(a^{1/n}\big)^{-m}\Big)^{-1}$
    83	  ([[lem-power-laws]], claim 2), the inverse of a positive element, hence positive
    84	  ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]). Note that [[lem-power-monotone]] is stated for
    85	  natural exponents only, so it does not by itself settle the negative case; that
    86	  is what the inverse step is for. The exponent laws are
    87	  [[lem-rational-power-laws]] and the order behaviour is
    88	  [[lem-rational-power-monotone]].
    89	- **The exponent is a rational, never a real.** Nothing on this page is a limit,
    90	  a series or a continuous function, and $a^{r}$ is computed in finitely many
    91	  field operations once the root is available. What would be needed to go
    92	  further, why it is deferred here, and where the library later defines $a^{x}$
    93	  for real $x$ are recorded in [[rem-real-exponents-deferred]].
    94	- The convention $0^0 = 1$ of [[def-integer-power]] is untouched: $r = 0$ is not
    95	  covered by the supplementary clause, which asks for $r > 0$. So $0^0 = 1$ while
    96	  $0^{r} = 0$ for every rational $r > 0$. There is no inconsistency, only the
    97	  familiar fact that the two-variable function $(a, r) \mapsto a^{r}$ has no
    98	  continuous extension to $(0,0)$, a statement this library cannot even make yet.
