    38	
    39	What this page has built is $a^{r}$ for a positive real base $a$ and a
    40	**rational** exponent $r$ ([[def-rational-power]]), together with its algebra
    41	([[lem-rational-power-laws]]) and its order behaviour: strictly increasing in
    42	$r$ for $a > 1$, constant for $a = 1$, strictly decreasing for $0 < a < 1$, and
    43	strictly increasing in $a$ for a fixed positive exponent
    44	([[lem-rational-power-monotone]]). Nothing here is a limit, a series or a
    45	continuous function. Every value is produced by finitely many field operations
    46	once the relevant root is available, and the only nonalgebraic ingredient
    47	anywhere on the page is the least-upper-bound property
    48	([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). It enters exactly one proof on the page
    49	directly, the existence of $n$-th roots ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]); everything
    50	else here that needs a root cites a theorem rather than the property itself, and
    51	those theorems are [[thm-nth-roots-exist]] and, for the root form of
    52	Cauchy-Schwarz ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]]), the already published
    53	[[thm-of-square-roots]]. In each case completeness is spent on producing a root
    54	and on nothing else.
    55	
    56	**The obvious next step, and how far it gets.** For $a > 1$ and a real $x$, put
    57	
    58	$$E_a(x) := \sup\{\, a^{r} : r \in \mathbb{Q},\; r \le x \,\}.$$
    59	
    60	This supremum exists. The set is nonempty because there is a rational below $x$,
    61	and it is bounded above because there is a rational $R > x$, whence
    62	$a^{r} \le a^{R}$ for every rational $r \le x$ by monotonicity in the exponent;
    63	both rationals are supplied by the density of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$
    64	([[lem-of-q-dense]], [[thm-of-archimedean]]). The definition is also consistent
    65	with what we already have: for a rational $x$ the set has greatest element
    66	$a^{x}$, and a set with a greatest element has that element as its supremum
    67	([[lem-max-is-sup]]), so $E_a(x) = a^{x}$. For $0 < a < 1$ one sets
    68	$E_a(x) := 1/E_{1/a}(x)$, and $E_1(x) := 1$. So the object exists, it is the
    69	right object, and it is monotone.
    70	
    71	**Where it stops.** Proving the law $E_a(x+y) = E_a(x)E_a(y)$ from this
    72	definition is not an algebraic manipulation. One has to compare a supremum over
    73	rationals $r \le x + y$ with the products $a^{s}a^{t}$ for $s \le x$, $t \le y$,
    74	and the two families are not the same: every $s + t$ is one of the $r$, but a
    75	given $r \le x+y$ need only be *approximated* by such sums. Closing that gap is
    76	an approximation argument, and an approximation argument needs a notion of limit
    77	and an estimate that controls $a^{r} - a^{s}$ in terms of $r - s$. The library
    78	does have a notion of convergent sequence of reals, introduced in the
    79	construction of $\mathbb{R}$ via Cauchy sequences ([[def-real-limit]]), but no
    80	continuity, no uniform continuity, no series and no derivative is available at
    81	this point in its reading order, and each of those is exactly what the standard
    82	proofs of the power laws for real exponents use. Writing such
    83	a proof here would either import machinery that does not exist yet or quietly
    84	assume it, and the second is the failure mode this library is built to avoid.
    85	
    86	**The route the library will actually take.** General powers will be defined not
    87	by the supremum above but through the exponential function and the logarithm,
    88	via $a^{x} = \exp(x \log a)$ for $a > 0$. That route is developed much later,
    89	after limits, continuity, series and differentiation are in place; the
    90	exponential is built as a power series or as the solution of a differential
    91	equation, the logarithm as its inverse, and the power laws for real exponents
    92	then fall out of the functional equation $\exp(u+v) = \exp(u)\exp(v)$ instead of
    93	being fought for one at a time. The supremum definition is then recovered as a
    94	theorem rather than taken as a definition. That later development is now
    95	published: [[def-real-power]] defines $a^{x} := \exp(x \log a)$ for $a > 0$ and
    96	real $x$, and [[thm-real-power-laws]] proves the power laws for those exponents.
    97	
    98	**Practical consequence for this page.** Everywhere an exponent appears in a
    99	statement on this page it is an integer or a rational, and where that looks like
   100	a restriction it is a real one. The weights in the weighted AM-GM inequality are
   101	rational for this reason, and the conjugate exponent $q = p/(p-1)$ in Hölder and
   102	Minkowski is rational precisely because $p$ is. Those statements are not weaker
   103	versions of the classical ones waiting to be upgraded; they are the classical
   104	ones, restricted to the exponents that currently have a meaning.
