    28	
    29	## Statement
    30	
    31	For $a,b>0$ and $r,s\in\mathbb R$,
    32	$$a^{r+s}=a^ra^s,\qquad (ab)^r=a^rb^r,\qquad (a/b)^r=a^r/b^r,\qquad (a^r)^s=a^{rs}.$$
    33	
    34	## Facts & Assumptions
    35	
    36	**Given:** Positive reals $a,b$ and real exponents $r,s$.
    37	
    38	[L1] $a^u=\exp(u\log a)$ for $a>0$ ([[def-real-power]]).
    39	
    40	[L2] $\log(ab)=\log a+\log b$, $\log(a/b)=\log a-\log b$, and $\log(\exp u)=u$ ([[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]).
    41	
    42	[L3] $\exp(u+v)=\exp(u)\exp(v)$ and $\exp(-u)=1/\exp(u)$ ([[thm-exponential-addition-formula]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]]).
    43	
    44	
    45	
    46	## Proof
    47	
    48	**Proof technique:** direct.
    49	
    50	1.1 Expanding $a^{r+s}$ by [L1] and applying [L3] gives $a^{r+s}=\exp(r\log a)\exp(s\log a)=a^ra^s$. [L1, L3]
    51	
    52	1.2 Expanding $(ab)^r$ and using $\log(ab)=\log a+\log b$ gives $(ab)^r=a^rb^r$. [L1, L2, L3]
    53	
    54	1.3 The same calculation with $\log(a/b)=\log a-\log b$ and [L3] gives $(a/b)^r=a^r/b^r$. [L1, L2, L3]
    55	
    56	2.1 Since $\log(a^r)=\log(\exp(r\log a))=r\log a$, expanding $(a^r)^s$ gives $a^{rs}$. [L1, L2] ∎

