    33	
    34	Five convention forks are live in the material of this page, and each is settled
    35	here rather than left to the reader. Two further conventions are inherited and
    36	change how statements here are read.
    37	
    38	**1. The empty space is connected, and so is a one-point space.** A separation
    39	asks for two nonempty disjoint open pieces covering the space
    40	([[def-connected-space]]), and neither space admits one, so both are connected
    41	with no special clause. The competing convention
    42	adds "nonempty" to the definition of a connected space, which makes the empty
    43	space neither connected nor disconnected. The cost of the choice made here is
    44	that the empty set is a connected subset of every space, so "maximal connected
    45	subset" must be read as "maximal among the nonempty connected subsets" for the
    46	components; that is exactly where
    47	[[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]] and the maximality clause of the
    48	components theorem take care. The benefit is that no theorem on this page needs a
    49	nonemptiness hypothesis: unions, closures, continuous images and products are all
    50	stated without one.
    51	
    52	**2. A separation is two disjoint open sets; separated sets are a different
    53	condition, and both are used.** [[def-connected-space]] defines a separation of a
    54	space by open sets, and defines $A_1, A_2$ to be *separated in $X$* when neither
    55	meets the other's closure. Those are not the same demand: separated sets need not
    56	be open, and the ambient open sets that witness a separation of a subspace need
    57	not be disjoint in $X$ at all — [[def-connected-space]] requires them to be
    58	disjoint only *on* the subspace, and "requiring $U \cap V = \varnothing$ outright
    59	is a strictly stronger demand and is a different notion".
    60	[[lem-connected-subsets-and-separated-sets]] is the theorem
    61	relating them, and it is what lets a computation be done in whichever of the two
    62	vocabularies is convenient. The real-line development uses the second
    63	([[def-connected-r]]), the general development uses the first, and
    64	[[cor-connected-subsets-of-the-line]] is where the two are shown to agree on
    65	$\mathbb{R}$ — an identification that is proved, never assumed.
    66	
    67	**3. Local connectedness demands OPEN connected sets.** [[def-locally-connected]]
    68	asks that every open $U$ containing $x$ contain an *open* connected $V$ with
    69	$x \in V \subseteq U$. Asking instead only that $U$ contain a connected $V$ that
    70	is a *neighbourhood* of $x$, without requiring $V$ itself to be open, gives a
    71	weaker condition at a point, called *connectedness im kleinen* in the
    72	literature; dropping *open* outright, so that any connected $V$ with
    73	$x \in V \subseteq U$ would serve, asks nothing at all, since the singleton
    74	$\{x\}$ always qualifies. This page proves
    75	nothing about that weaker condition and asserts no relation between the two. The
    76	same fork, with the same resolution, applies to local path-connectedness
    77	([[def-path-connected]]).
    78	
    79	**4. Totally disconnected is defined by components, not by quasicomponents.**
    80	[[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]] calls $X$ totally disconnected
    81	when every component is a singleton. The condition that every *quasicomponent* is
    82	a singleton is a different property, usually called total separatedness, and by
    83	[[thm-quasicomponents-contain-components]] it is at least as strong. Nothing on
    84	this page asserts that the two agree.
    85	
    86	**5. The order topology is generated by the open rays.**
    87	[[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]] takes the rays $L_{<a}$ and
    88	$L_{>a}$ as a subbasis, which is what makes the definition work uniformly when
    89	$L$ has a least or a greatest element; taking the open intervals alone as a basis
    90	would fail there. The same item fixes *order-convex*, *order-dense*, the *least
    91	upper bound property* and *linear continuum*, and records that a subspace of a
    92	linearly ordered topological space always means the subspace topology, which
    93	agrees with the order topology of the restricted order when the subset is
    94	order-convex and is not claimed to agree otherwise.
    95	
    96	**Two inherited conventions that change how this page is read.** A neighbourhood
    97	need not be open, so "open connected neighbourhood" is written out in full
    98	wherever openness is wanted; and the empty intersection of a subbasis is the
    99	whole space, so no covering hypothesis is imposed on the subbasis of rays. These
   100	conventions are in force throughout the items above.
   101	
   102	**Notation used without further comment.** $\mathbf{2}$ is the two-point discrete
   103	space ([[def-standard-topologies]]); $C(x)$, $Q(x)$ and $P(x)$ are the component,
   104	the quasicomponent and the path component of $x$; and "interval" applied to a
   105	subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is read throughout as "order-convex", the classification
   106	of the order-convex subsets into written forms not being available here.
