    37	
    38	## Definition
    39	
    40	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
    41	
    42	- A **separation** of $X$ is an ordered pair $(U, V)$ of **open**, **nonempty**,
    43	  **disjoint** subsets of $X$ with $U \cup V = X$.
    44	- $X$ is **disconnected** when a separation of $X$ exists, and **connected** when
    45	  none does.
    46	- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **connected subset** of $X$ when the space
    47	  $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is connected, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the subspace topology
    48	  ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). "Disconnected subset" is read the same way.
    49	
    50	Since $U$ and $V$ are complementary in $X$, each of them is closed as well as
    51	open; so a separation is the same thing as a partition of $X$ into two nonempty
    52	clopen pieces ([[def-topological-space]]). The **clopen** subsets of $X$ are
    53	those that are both open and closed, and $\varnothing$ and $X$ are always among
    54	them.
    55	
    56	**The empty space and the one-point space are connected in this library.**
    57	Neither admits a separation: a separation requires two nonempty disjoint sets
    58	whose union is the whole space, and neither $\varnothing$ nor a singleton can be
    59	written as such a union. So both are connected under the definition above,
    60	without any special clause. **This is a live convention fork** and the competing
    61	choice is recorded in [[rem-connectedness-conventions]]; nothing on this page
    62	depends on which is taken except the reading of the word "connected" applied to
    63	those two spaces.
    64	
    65	**Connectedness is a property of a space, not of an ambient pair.** The condition
    66	above mentions only $(X,\mathcal{T})$. When it is applied to $A \subseteq X$ it
    67	is applied to the space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$, so it does not change if $A$ is
    68	regarded as a subspace of some other space inducing the same topology on $A$; in
    69	particular a subset of $A$ is connected as a subset of $A$ exactly when it is
    70	connected as a subset of $X$, by transitivity of the subspace topology
    71	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). This is why "connected" may be used of a subset
    72	with no ambient space named.
    73	
    74	**Spelled out for a subset.** $A \subseteq X$ is disconnected exactly when there
    75	are open $U, V \subseteq X$ with
    76	
    77	$$A \subseteq U \cup V, \qquad U \cap A \ne \varnothing, \qquad V \cap A \ne \varnothing, \qquad U \cap V \cap A = \varnothing,$$
    78	
    79	because the open sets of $(A,\mathcal{T}_A)$ are precisely the traces $U \cap A$.
    80	Note the last condition: it asks $U$ and $V$ to be disjoint **on $A$**, not in
    81	$X$. Requiring $U \cap V = \varnothing$ outright is a strictly stronger demand
    82	and is a different notion.
    83	
    84	**The two-point discrete space.** Write $\mathbf{2} := \{0,1\}$ with the discrete
    85	topology ([[def-standard-topologies]]), in which every subset is open. A
    86	separation of $X$ is the same datum as a surjective continuous map
    87	$X \to \mathbf{2}$ ([[def-continuous-map-top]]): given $(U,V)$, the map sending
    88	$U$ to $0$ and $V$ to $1$ is continuous because the preimage of each of the four
    89	open subsets of $\mathbf{2}$ is one of $\varnothing$, $U$, $V$, $X$; given a
    90	surjective continuous $\chi : X \to \mathbf{2}$, the pair
    91	$(\chi^{-1}[\{0\}], \chi^{-1}[\{1\}])$ is a separation. This reformulation is
    92	proved as a theorem on this page and is recorded here only to name $\mathbf{2}$.
    93	
    94	**Separated sets.** Two subsets $A_1, A_2 \subseteq X$ are **separated in $X$**
    95	when
    96	
    97	$$\overline{A_1} \cap A_2 = \varnothing \qquad \text{and} \qquad A_1 \cap \overline{A_2} = \varnothing,$$
    98	
    99	closures taken in $X$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]],
   100	[[thm-closure-characterisation-top]]). Separated sets are disjoint, since
   101	$A_1 \subseteq \overline{A_1}$; the converse fails. This is verbatim the
   102	condition [[def-connected-r]] uses on the real line, transported to an arbitrary
   103	space, and the theorem relating it to the definition above is the next lemma on
   104	this page.
   105	
   106	**Totally disconnected spaces, and the empty case.** The vocabulary for a space
   107	all of whose connected subsets are single points is fixed later on this page,
   108	together with the components; it is not defined here because it is stated in
   109	terms of components.
   110	
   111	## Remarks
   112	
   113	- **Why "nonempty" and "disjoint" are both in the definition.** Drop
   114	  *nonempty* and every space with more than one open set is "disconnected" via
   115	  $(X, \varnothing)$. Drop *disjoint* and $(X, X)$ separates every nonempty
   116	  space. Drop *open* and every space with at least two points is separated by a
   117	  point and its complement. Each of the four conditions is doing work, and the
   118	  four together are the weakest demand under which the notion has the
   119	  consequences proved on this page.
   120	
   121	- **Ordered pair, not unordered.** A separation is written as a pair for
   122	  convenience only; $(U,V)$ separates exactly when $(V,U)$ does, and no statement
   123	  here distinguishes them.
   124	
   125	- **The relation to the real-line definition is proved, not assumed.**
   126	  [[def-connected-r]] defines connectedness of $E \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ by the
   127	  absence of a partition into two nonempty separated sets, which is a condition
   128	  on closures rather than on relatively open sets. That the two definitions agree
   129	  is [[lem-connected-subsets-and-separated-sets]] together with
   130	  [[cor-connected-subsets-of-the-line]]; until those are proved, the two words
   131	  are kept apart and no statement here quietly identifies them.
