    36	---
    37	
    38	## Definition
    39	
    40	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
    41	$x \in X$. Subsets carry the subspace topology
    42	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]); connectedness is [[def-connected-space]] and
    43	path-connectedness is [[def-path-connected]].
    44	
    45	- $X$ is **locally connected at $x$** when for every open $U$ with $x \in U$
    46	  there is an **open connected** $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$.
    47	- $X$ is **locally connected** when it is locally connected at every point.
    48	- $X$ is **locally path-connected at $x$** when for every open $U$ with
    49	  $x \in U$ there is an **open path-connected** $V$ with $x \in V \subseteq U$;
    50	  and **locally path-connected** when this holds at every point.
    51	
    52	**The neighbourhood-base reading.** $X$ is locally connected at $x$ exactly when
    53	the open connected sets containing $x$ form a neighbourhood base at $x$
    54	([[def-neighbourhood-top]]). Indeed a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$ contains an open
    55	$U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$, and an open connected $V$ with
    56	$x \in V \subseteq U$ is then a member of that family inside $N$; conversely a
    57	base member inside an open $U \ni x$ is exactly what the displayed condition
    58	asks. The same sentence with "path-connected" in place of "connected" gives the
    59	reading for local path-connectedness. Recall that in this library a neighbourhood
    60	need not be open ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]), which is why "open" is written out
    61	in both clauses above.
    62	
    63	**Openness in the clauses is not removable and is a live fork.** Asking only for
    64	a *connected neighbourhood* inside every open $U \ni x$ — with no openness
    65	demanded of the connected set — defines an a priori weaker condition at a single
    66	point, called *connectedness im kleinen at $x$* in the literature. This library
    67	takes the definition above, with openness, and **no statement here asserts that
    68	the two agree**, at a point or globally.
    69	
    70	**Local and global connectedness are independent conditions, and neither clause
    71	above mentions the other.** A two-point discrete space
    72	([[def-standard-topologies]]) is locally connected, every singleton being open
    73	and connected, and is not connected, the two singletons separating it. So local
    74	connectedness does not imply connectedness. The reverse implication is not
    75	asserted here either.
    76	
    77	**Both notions are properties of the space, not of an ambient pair.** "A locally
    78	connected subset $A \subseteq X$" means that the space $A$ with its subspace
    79	topology is locally connected, and the open sets tested are then the sets open in
    80	$A$.
    81	
    82	## Remarks
    83	
    84	- **Why the notion is stated at a point and then quantified.** Almost every
    85	  application needs the pointwise form: a space can fail to be locally connected
    86	  at a single point and be perfectly well behaved everywhere else, and naming the
    87	  bad point is what a counterexample does. Quantifying afterwards costs one line
    88	  and keeps both forms available.
    89	
    90	- **The relation to components.** The condition says that arbitrarily small open
    91	  connected sets exist around each point. Since the component of $x$ inside an
    92	  open $U$ is the largest connected subset of $U$ containing $x$
    93	  ([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]]), the definition is asking that
    94	  those components be large enough to be neighbourhoods — which is exactly the
    95	  reformulation proved as the next item on this page.
    96	
    97	- **Local path-connectedness is strictly the stronger-looking of the two, and
    98	  nothing here compares them.** Every path-connected space is connected, so an
    99	  open path-connected set is an open connected set and local path-connectedness
   100	  implies local connectedness once that implication is available; it is proved
   101	  later on this page and is not assumed in this definition.
