    39	
    40	## Definition
    41	
    42	Throughout, $I := [0,1] = \{\, t \in \mathbb{R} : 0 \le t \le 1 \,\}$
    43	([[def-interval]]) carries the subspace topology inherited from $\mathbb{R}$ with
    44	its usual topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]],
    45	[[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
    46	[[def-metrizable-space]]). It is called the **unit interval**.
    47	
    48	Let $X$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
    49	$x, y \in X$.
    50	
    51	- A **path in $X$ from $x$ to $y$** is a continuous map $\gamma : I \to X$
    52	  ([[def-continuous-map-top]]) with $\gamma(0) = x$ and $\gamma(1) = y$. Its
    53	  **image** is $\gamma[I]$.
    54	- $X$ is **path-connected** when for every pair $x, y \in X$ there is a path in
    55	  $X$ from $x$ to $y$. A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **path-connected subset**
    56	  when the space $A$ with its subspace topology is path-connected; equivalently,
    57	  when any two of its points are joined by a path whose image lies in $A$, by the
    58	  characteristic property of a map into a subspace
    59	  ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    60	- Write $x \sim y$ when a path in $X$ from $x$ to $y$ exists. The **path
    61	  component** of $x$ is its equivalence class
    62	  $$P(x) \;:=\; \{\, y \in X : x \sim y \,\} .$$
    63	- The empty space is path-connected, the defining condition quantifying over no
    64	  pair of points, and so is every one-point space, the constant path joining its
    65	  point to itself.
    66	
    67	**$\sim$ is an equivalence relation on $X$, and the obligation is discharged
    68	here**, so that "equivalence class" above denotes.
    69	
    70	*Reflexive.* The constant map $\gamma(t) = x$ is continuous, every preimage being
    71	$\varnothing$ or $I$ ([[def-continuous-map-top]]), and joins $x$ to $x$.
    72	
    73	*Symmetric.* If $\gamma$ joins $x$ to $y$, put $\bar\gamma(t) := \gamma(1-t)$.
    74	The map $r : I \to I$, $r(t) = 1 - t$, is continuous: for $s, t \in I$ one has
    75	$|r(s) - r(t)| = |s - t|$, so a ball of radius $\varepsilon$ around $r(t)$ pulls
    76	back to contain the ball of radius $\varepsilon$ around $t$
    77	([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-metrizable-space]]). Hence
    78	$\bar\gamma = \gamma \circ r$ is continuous ([[lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes]],
    79	claim 1) and joins $y$ to $x$.
    80	
    81	*Transitive.* Let $\gamma_1$ join $x$ to $y$ and $\gamma_2$ join $y$ to $z$.
    82	Define $\delta : I \to X$ by
    83	
    84	$$\delta(t) \;:=\; \begin{cases} \gamma_1(2t), & 0 \le t \le 1/2, \\ \gamma_2(2t - 1), & 1/2 \le t \le 1. \end{cases}$$
    85	
    86	The two clauses agree at $t = 1/2$, both giving $\gamma_1(1) = y = \gamma_2(0)$,
    87	so $\delta$ is a well-defined function. The sets $[0,1/2]$ and $[1/2,1]$ are
    88	closed in $I$ and cover it, and there are **two** of them, so the finite closed
    89	form of the pasting lemma applies ([[lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes]],
    90	claim 3). On $[0,1/2]$ the map $\delta$ is $\gamma_1 \circ a_1$ with
    91	$a_1(t) = 2t$, and on $[1/2,1]$ it is $\gamma_2 \circ a_2$ with
    92	$a_2(t) = 2t - 1$; each $a_k$ is continuous into $I$, since
    93	$|a_k(s) - a_k(t)| = 2|s-t|$, so the ball of radius $\varepsilon/2$ around $t$
    94	maps into the ball of radius $\varepsilon$ around $a_k(t)$
    95	([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-metrizable-space]],
    96	[[def-subspace-topology-top]]). So both restrictions are continuous by
    97	[[lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes]] claim 1, hence $\delta$ is continuous, and
    98	it joins $x$ to $z$.
    99	
   100	**The path components partition $X$**, being the classes of an equivalence
   101	relation, and each is a path-connected subset of $X$: two points of $P(x)$ are
   102	joined to $x$, hence to each other by the transitivity construction above, and
   103	the resulting path has image inside $P(x)$: if $\delta$ is a path from $x$ and
   104	$s \in I$, then $t \mapsto \delta(st)$ is a path from $x$ to $\delta(s)$,
   105	continuous because $t \mapsto st$ satisfies $|st_1 - st_2| \le |t_1 - t_2|$ and
   106	is therefore continuous into $I$ by the ball criterion used above, so every point
   107	of the image is itself joined to $x$.
   108	
   109	## Remarks
   110	
   111	- **Why the unit interval and not an arbitrary closed bounded interval.** Any
   112	  $[a,b]$ with $a < b$ would give the same relation, since $t \mapsto a + t(b-a)$
   113	  carries $[0,1]$ onto $[a,b]$ and is continuous with continuous inverse. Fixing
   114	  $[0,1]$ removes a parameter from every statement below and costs nothing.
   115	
   116	- **A path is a map, not a subset.** The image $\gamma[I]$ is a subset of $X$,
   117	  but the path is the map: two different paths may have the same image, and the
   118	  concatenation above depends on the maps rather than on their images. Nothing
   119	  in this library identifies a path with its image.
   120	
   121	- **Path components are not asserted to be closed, or open, or to coincide with
   122	  components.** Each of those is false in general, and each is taken up
   123	  separately on this page. What is proved here is only that they partition $X$
   124	  and that each is path-connected.
   125	
   126	- **The finiteness in the pasting lemma is what makes concatenation legal.** The
   127	  cover $\{[0,1/2], [1/2,1]\}$ has two members. An infinite closed cover would
   128	  not do, and the standing warning is
   129	  [[cex-pasting-fails-for-an-infinite-closed-cover]]; this is worth naming here
   130	  because the temptation to concatenate infinitely many paths is exactly what
   131	  fails for the zigzag curve later on this page.

