    31	
    32	## Definition
    33	
    34	Let $A, B, E \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, with closure as in
    35	[[def-interior-closure-boundary-r]].
    36	
    37	- $A$ and $B$ are **separated** when
    38	  $$\overline{A} \cap B = \varnothing \quad \text{and} \quad A \cap \overline{B} = \varnothing .$$
    39	- A **disconnection** of $E$ is a pair $(A,B)$ of nonempty separated sets with
    40	  $E = A \cup B$.
    41	- $E$ is **disconnected** when it admits a disconnection, and **connected** when
    42	  it does not.
    43	
    44	**Separated is strictly stronger than disjoint.** Since $A \subseteq
    45	\overline{A}$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-r]]), the first displayed
    46	condition already gives $A \cap B \subseteq \overline{A} \cap B = \varnothing$,
    47	so separated sets are disjoint. The converse fails: $(0,1)$ and $[1,2)$ are
    48	disjoint, yet every neighbourhood of $1$ meets $(0,1)$, so $1$ is an adherent
    49	point of $(0,1)$ and lies in $\overline{(0,1)}$
    50	([[thm-closure-characterisations-r]]), while $1 \in [1,2)$; hence
    51	$\overline{(0,1)} \cap [1,2) \ne \varnothing$ and the pair is not separated.
    52	What separation adds to disjointness is exactly this: **neither set of a
    53	separated pair may contain a point adherent to the other**, which is what makes
    54	a disconnection a genuine splitting rather than a bookkeeping partition.
    55	
    56	**Separation does not ask the two closures to be disjoint.** Each condition
    57	tests one closure against the *other set*, never closure against closure. The
    58	pair $(0,1)$, $(1,2)$ illustrates the difference and is separated: $[0,1]$ is a
    59	closed set containing $(0,1)$, so $\overline{(0,1)} \subseteq [0,1]$
    60	([[def-open-and-closed-in-r]], [[def-interior-closure-boundary-r]]) and
    61	$\overline{(0,1)} \cap (1,2) = \varnothing$; symmetrically
    62	$\overline{(1,2)} \subseteq [1,2]$ and $(0,1) \cap \overline{(1,2)} =
    63	\varnothing$. The two closures nevertheless share the point $1$, so a definition
    64	demanding $\overline{A} \cap \overline{B} = \varnothing$ would be a different and
    65	strictly stronger condition, and it is not the one used here.
    66	
    67	## Remarks
    68	
    69	- **Why separation and not "both pieces open".** For a subset $E$ of
    70	  $\mathbb{R}$ the pieces of a splitting are rarely open as subsets of
    71	  $\mathbb{R}$: in the disconnection of $\mathbb{Q} \cap [0,2]$ used by
    72	  [[cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected]] neither piece is open in
    73	  $\mathbb{R}$. Rudin's separated-sets formulation avoids introducing a second
    74	  topology relative to $E$, and it is the only formulation this page uses.
    75	  Nothing below refers to sets open "in $E$".
    76	
    77	- **Every one-point set and the empty set are connected.** A disconnection
    78	  requires two nonempty pieces with union $E$, and if $E$ has at most one point
    79	  no two nonempty disjoint sets have union $E$.
    80	
    81	- **Connectedness of a subset of $\mathbb{R}$ turns out to be an order
    82	  property**: $E$ is connected exactly when it is order-convex
    83	  ([[thm-connected-subsets-of-r-are-intervals]]). That is a theorem about
    84	  $\mathbb{R}$ and uses its completeness; the definition above mentions no order
    85	  at all.
