research/audit/wave3-real-analysis.proof-contracts.json:6068:     "id": "lem-rational-power-laws::3.3",
research/audit/wave3-real-analysis.proof-contracts.json-6069-     "step": "3.3",
research/audit/wave3-real-analysis.proof-contracts.json-6070-     "claim": "Claim 5: write $r = m/n$ and $s = p/q$ with $n, q \\ge 1$, and put $x := a^{1/(nq)}$, so $x > 0$ and $x^{nq} = a$; then $\\big(x^{q}\\big)^{n} = x^{qn} = a$ with $x^{q} > 0$, so $x^{q} = a^{1/n}$ by uniqueness of the nonnegative $n$-th root; putting $z := x^{m}$ we get $z > 0$ and $z^{q} = \\big(x^{m}\\big)^{q} = \\big(x^{q}\\big)^{m} = \\big(a^{1/n}\\big)^{m} = a^{r}$, so $z$ is the nonnegative $q$-th root of $a^{r}$, that is $z = \\big(a^{r}\\big)^{1/q}$; therefore $\\big(a^{r}\\big)^{s} = \\Big(\\big(a^{r}\\big)^{1/q}\\Big)^{p} = z^{p} = \\big(x^{m}\\big)^{p} = x^{mp} = \\big(a^{1/(nq)}\\big)^{mp} = a^{(mp)/(nq)} = a^{rs}$.",
