research/audit/wave3-judge-adjudications.jsonl:121:{"id":"rem-connectedness-conventions","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"7e82a3396629e85e82ce461b0cef63c77200c00f5d74021d1d19dbc67bd49e7a","item_sha256":"306d5a3ce3a2e84131d23d2dc2a8914675c57a64d519ea3e5067e54b7cd92704","outcome":"confirmed_fatal","defect_type":"other","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","round":2,"note":"New finding on the A8-repaired text, in section 2 rather than the repaired section 3, and Terra is right. Section 2 claims 'disjoint open sets in a subspace need not be separated in the ambient space'. They always are: if U, V are disjoint and open in S, write V = W ∩ S with W open in X; then W ∩ U ⊆ W ∩ S = V and U ∩ V = ∅ give W ∩ U = ∅, so V misses the closure of U in X, and symmetrically. The item's own cited lem-connected-subsets-and-separated-sets runs exactly that argument at its step 3.1. The true distinction, which def-connected-space states, is that the AMBIENT open sets witnessing a separation of a subspace need only be disjoint ON the subspace: 'it asks U and V to be disjoint on A, not in X. Requiring U ∩ V = ∅ outright is a strictly stronger demand and is a different notion.' Repaired to state that."}

