     1	---
     2	page: roots-and-rational-powers
     3	title: "Roots, Rational Powers, and Classical Inequalities"
     4	status: published
     5	items: [def-finite-sum, lem-finite-sum-laws, def-integer-power, lem-power-laws,
     6	        lem-power-monotone, lem-bernoulli-inequality,
     7	        lem-power-difference-factorisation, thm-nth-roots-exist,
     8	        def-rational-power, lem-rational-power-well-defined,
     9	        lem-rational-power-laws, lem-rational-power-monotone,
    10	        lem-triangle-inequality-finite, thm-am-gm, thm-weighted-am-gm-rational,
    11	        thm-young-inequality, thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite, thm-holder-finite,
    12	        thm-minkowski-finite, rem-real-exponents-deferred]
    13	examples: [fs-negative-has-square-root, fs-rational-power-of-negative-base]
    14	---
    15	
    16	**Objective.** This page builds exponentiation inside $\mathbb{R}$ as far as it
    17	can honestly be built with the tools the library currently owns, and then proves
    18	the classical inequalities that live at that level. The tools are the field
    19	axioms, the order, and the least-upper-bound property
    20	([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). No proof on this page uses the exponential
    21	function, logarithm, continuity, or infinite series; a limit of a sequence of
    22	reals is defined in the Cauchy construction ([[def-real-limit]]), but no proof
    23	on this page uses one. So **every exponent on this page is an integer or a
    24	rational, and every proof is a finite algebraic argument plus, in exactly one
    25	proof, the least-upper-bound property.**
    26	
    27	The construction runs in three stages. First, finite sums and finite products
    28	are defined by recursion ([[def-finite-sum]], resting on [[thm-recursion]]) and
    29	their laws are proved by induction, because "$a_0 + \cdots + a_{n-1}$" is
    30	notation and not a definition. Second, integer powers are defined by the same
    31	mechanism and their algebra and order behaviour established. Third, roots are
    32	produced: for $a \ge 0$ and $n \ge 1$ there is a unique nonnegative $s$ with
    33	$s^{n} = a$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]). That theorem is the one proof on this page
    34	that invokes the least-upper-bound property directly, and it is a
    35	**generalisation of the already published
    36	[[thm-of-square-roots]]**: the case $n = 2$ is not new here and is not claimed
    37	to be. What is new is the passage to general $n$, where the completed square
    38	that drives the square-root argument is replaced by the factorisation of
    39	$b^{n} - a^{n}$ and the Lipschitz estimate it yields
    40	([[lem-power-difference-factorisation]]).
    41	
    42	Rational powers follow: $a^{r} := \big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m}$ for $a > 0$ and
    43	$r = m/n$ ([[def-rational-power]]). Two things about that definition deserve
    44	attention. Its **well-definedness is a theorem**, not an observation: different
    45	representatives of the same rational must give the same value, and
    46	[[lem-rational-power-well-defined]] proves they do, which is why it is recorded
    47	in the definition's `justified_by` rather than in its `deps`. And the
    48	restriction to a **positive base is forced**, not stylistic:
    49	[[fs-rational-power-of-negative-base]] shows that for $a < 0$ the very same
    50	formula assigns $-2$ to $(-8)^{1/3}$ and nothing at all to $(-8)^{2/6}$, though
    51	$1/3$ and $2/6$ are the same rational.
    52	
    53	The inequalities then follow one from the next. AM-GM ([[thm-am-gm]]) is proved
    54	by Cauchy's forward-backward induction, up a doubling sequence of **natural
    55	numbers** and then downward, with the equality case tracked throughout. The
    56	doubling sequence is defined by recursion inside $\mathbb{N}$ rather than written
    57	$2^{j}$, because a list length must be a natural number and $2^{j}$ would name a
    58	real ([[def-integer-power]] gives powers of a real base only). Its weighted form
    59	([[thm-weighted-am-gm-rational]]) is stated for **rational** weights, and that
    60	restriction is not laziness: a real weight $w$ would require the symbol $x^{w}$,
    61	which does not exist in this library. Young's inequality
    62	([[thm-young-inequality]]) is the two-weight case, Hölder
    63	([[thm-holder-finite]]) is Young summed after normalisation, and Minkowski
    64	([[thm-minkowski-finite]]) is Hölder applied twice with the conjugate exponent
    65	$q = p/(p-1)$, **which is rational exactly because $p$ is**. Cauchy-Schwarz
    66	([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]]) is proved separately and by a route that uses no
    67	root at all, from the nonnegativity of $\sum_k (a_k t + b_k)^{2}$; its root form
    68	is a restatement, reached through the already published [[thm-of-square-roots]]
    69	rather than through the $n$-th roots of this page.
    70	Bernoulli's inequality ([[lem-bernoulli-inequality]]) sits outside that chain: it
    71	is proved here as a classical inequality of exactly this level, for its own sake
    72	rather than as a step towards the others, and nothing else on this page rests on
    73	it.
    74	
    75	Two false statements guard the boundary of what has been built. No real number
    76	squares to a negative one ([[fs-negative-has-square-root]]), in any ordered
    77	field and with no appeal to completeness, though odd roots of negatives do exist
    78	and the map $x \mapsto x^{n}$ is a bijection of $\mathbb{R}$ for odd $n$. And
    79	the rational-power formula does not survive the passage to negative bases
    80	([[fs-rational-power-of-negative-base]]). The closing remark
    81	([[rem-real-exponents-deferred]]) is honest about the ceiling: $a^{x}$ for real
    82	$x$ can be defined here as $\sup\{a^{r} : r \in \mathbb{Q},\ r \le x\}$, and that
    83	supremum does exist, but the power laws for it cannot be proved without limits.
    84	General powers require further analytic development, through the exponential
    85	and logarithm, and are deliberately outside this page's dependency closure.
