    19	           fs-the-closure-of-a-path-connected-set-is-path-connected,
    20	           fs-a-connected-space-is-locally-connected,
    21	           fs-an-intersection-of-connected-sets-is-connected,
    22	           fs-a-totally-disconnected-space-is-discrete]
    23	---
    24	
    25	**Objective.** A space is connected when it cannot be split into two nonempty
    26	open pieces. That is the whole definition, and almost everything on this page is
    27	an attempt to say what it is good for and what it fails to guarantee. The
    28	organising fact is that connectedness is preserved by continuous maps, by unions
    29	through a common point, and by passing to a closure, while it is
    30	preserved by neither intersections nor complements, and implies nothing about how
    31	a space looks near any one of its points.
    32	
    33	**The order topology, minted here.** [[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]
    34	puts a topology on an arbitrary linearly ordered set, generated by the open rays
    35	$L_{<a}$ and $L_{>a}$ as a subbasis, and fixes the vocabulary that the last
    36	theorems of the page need: order-convex sets, order-density, the least upper
    37	bound property, and the **linear continuum** — a linearly ordered set with at
    38	least two elements having both of the last two. It records that the order
    39	topology of $\mathbb{R}$ is its usual topology, so no second topology on the line
    40	is introduced, and that an order-convex subset carries the same topology whether
    41	it is read as a subspace or given its own order topology.
    42	
    43	**Separations, and three ways to see one.** [[def-connected-space]] defines a
    44	separation as a pair of disjoint nonempty open sets covering the space, and
    45	records that the empty space and every one-point space are connected under that
    46	definition. [[thm-connectedness-characterisations]] then proves the working
    47	equivalences: no separation exists; the only clopen subsets are $\varnothing$ and
    48	$X$; every continuous map to the two-point discrete space is constant; and the
    49	only subsets with empty boundary are $\varnothing$ and $X$. The third is what
    50	makes the later proofs short, because it converts connectedness into a statement
    51	about functions. [[lem-connected-subsets-and-separated-sets]] translates the
    52	definition for a subspace into a condition stated entirely in the ambient space,
    53	using separated sets rather than relatively open ones, and
    54	[[cor-connected-subsets-of-the-line]] uses that translation to transport the
    55	published classification of the connected subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ into the
    56	general vocabulary: they are exactly the order-convex sets.
    57	
    58	**What connectedness survives.** [[thm-continuous-image-of-a-connected-space]]
    59	shows that a continuous image of a connected space is connected, so
    60	connectedness is a topological property; [[cor-intermediate-value-theorem-topological]]
    61	reads that off for real-valued maps and obtains the intermediate value theorem
    62	with no hypothesis on the domain beyond connectedness.
    63	[[thm-unions-of-connected-sets]] gives the two union principles, through a common
    64	point and through a common connected core, and [[thm-closure-of-a-connected-set]]
    65	shows that any set squeezed between a connected set and its closure is connected.
    66	[[thm-product-of-connected-spaces]] combines the last two: the points differing
    67	from a fixed base point in finitely many coordinates form a connected dense
    68	subset of a product, so the product is connected. The argument is carried out in
    69	ZF, and the Statement separates connectedness from the assertion that the product
    70	is nonempty, which for an infinite index set is the Axiom of Choice.
    71	
    72	**Decomposing a space that is not connected.**
    73	[[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]] introduces the component of a
    74	point, the union of all connected sets through it, and the quasicomponent, the
    75	intersection of all clopen sets containing it, together with total
    76	disconnectedness. [[thm-components-partition-and-are-closed]] proves that the
    77	components are the maximal connected subsets, that they partition the space, and
    78	that each is closed; they need not be open.
    79	[[thm-quasicomponents-contain-components]] proves that each component lies inside
    80	a quasicomponent, that quasicomponents are closed unions of components and also
    81	partition the space, and asserts no converse.
    82	
    83	**Local conditions, and paths.** [[def-path-connected]] defines a path as a
    84	continuous map on the unit interval, proves that being joined by a path is an
    85	equivalence relation — reflexive by a constant path, symmetric by reversal, and
    86	transitive by concatenating two paths over a **two-piece** closed cover — and
    87	defines the path components. [[def-locally-connected]] asks for a neighbourhood
    88	base of *open* connected, respectively open path-connected, sets at every point,
    89	and records that dropping the word *open* would define a strictly weaker
    90	condition at a point. [[thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open]]
    91	characterises local connectedness by the openness of the components of every open
    92	subspace, and deduces that in a locally connected space the components are
    93	clopen. [[thm-path-connected-implies-connected]] proves that path-connectedness
    94	implies connectedness, taking the union of the images of **all** paths from a
    95	fixed point so that no choice principle is used, and that path components refine
    96	components. [[thm-connected-and-locally-path-connected-implies-path-connected]]
    97	supplies the converse under a local hypothesis: in a locally path-connected space
    98	the path components are clopen and agree with the components, so a connected such
    99	space is path-connected.
   100	
   101	**Two spaces built to be difficult.**
   102	[[thm-a-linear-continuum-is-connected]] proves that a linear continuum is
   103	connected in its order topology, and so is every order-convex subset of it; the
   104	least upper bound property produces a supremum and order-density produces a point
   105	strictly above it, and each hypothesis is spent exactly once.
   106	[[def-the-long-line]] then builds the closed long ray as $\omega_1 \times [0,1)$
   107	under the lexicographic order, with half-open blocks so that no jump appears at a
   108	block boundary, and [[thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum]] proves
   109	it is a linear continuum, hence connected, and that every at most countable
   110	subset of it is bounded above under the Axiom of Countable Choice.
   111	[[lem-the-oscillating-zigzag-curve]] constructs the second difficult space with
   112	no transcendental function anywhere: the graph of the piecewise-linear map
   113	running between $0$ and $1$ on each interval $[1/(n+2), 1/(n+1)]$. Its graph is
   114	homeomorphic to $(0,1]$; its closure adds the segment $\{0\} \times [0,1]$; that
   115	closure is connected, is not path-connected because no path joins the segment to
   116	the graph, and is not locally connected at any point of the segment. Continuity
   117	of the map is checked on an **open** cover, two closed pieces at a time, because
   118	the closed pasting lemma is false for an infinite closed cover.
   119	
   120	**False statements.** A connected space need not be path-connected
   121	([[fs-a-connected-space-is-path-connected]]); the closure of a path-connected set
   122	need not be path-connected
   123	([[fs-the-closure-of-a-path-connected-set-is-path-connected]]), although the
   124	closure of a connected set is always connected; a connected space need not be locally
   125	connected ([[fs-a-connected-space-is-locally-connected]]); the intersection of
   126	two connected sets need not be connected, witnessed by two three-sided pieces of
   127	the boundary of a square ([[fs-an-intersection-of-connected-sets-is-connected]]);
   128	and a totally disconnected space need not be discrete, witnessed by the
   129	rationals ([[fs-a-totally-disconnected-space-is-discrete]]).
   130	[[rem-connectedness-conventions]] closes the page by settling the convention
   131	forks that are live here: the empty and one-point spaces, separations against
   132	separated sets, the openness demanded in local connectedness, total
   133	disconnectedness stated by components rather than quasicomponents, and the rays
   134	as the subbasis of the order topology. The worked spaces are on
   135	[[connectedness-examples|the companion examples page]].

